<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Udaipur</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Udaipur is a city in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, founded in 1559 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharana">Maharana</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udai-singh-ii">Udai Singh II</a> as the new capital of Mewar. Built around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-pichola">Lake Pichola</a> and other artificial lakes in the Aravalli hills, it was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisodia">Sisodia</a> Maharanas until 1949, whose City Palace and lake palaces earned it the name 'city of lakes'. It is now a major centre of tourism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/udaipur.jpg" alt="Udaipur" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Geri from Biel/Bienne, Schweiz · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Evening_view,_City_Palace,_Udaipur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Udaipur lies in the southernmost part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> border, on the south slope of the Aravalli Range, which separates it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thar-desert">Thar Desert</a>. It stands at 598 metres above sea level and covers 64 square kilometres. Its lakes form an interconnected system fed by the Berach river and its tributaries. The city lakes include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-pichola">Lake Pichola</a>, Fateh Sagar Lake, Swaroop Sagar Lake and Rang Sagar, and downstream lies Udaisagar Lake. Because of its lakes it is sometimes called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> of the East, and the British administrator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-tod">James Tod</a> called it the most romantic spot on the continent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Maharana <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udai-singh-ii">Udai Singh II</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisodia">Sisodia</a> clan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a> founded Udaipur in 1559, as the new capital of Mewar. He was the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharana-pratap">Maharana Pratap</a>. The area already held a trading town, Ayad, which had been the capital of Mewar from the 10th to the 12th centuries. After the rise of artillery warfare in the 16th century, Udai Singh decided during his exile at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumbhalgarh">Kumbhalgarh Fort</a> to move to a more secure site. Ayad was flood-prone, so he chose the ridge east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-pichola">Lake Pichola</a>. In the legend, a hermit he met while hunting blessed him and told him to build a palace there.</p><p>The Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> conquered Chittor in November 1567. To protect his territory, Udai Singh built a six-kilometre city wall with gates including Surajpole, Chandpole, Hathipole and Delhi Gate. The area within is still known as the old city. Udaipur remained the capital of Mewar until 1818, when Mewar became a British princely state, and Mewar became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s independence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Udaipur is the administrative headquarters of Udaipur district, about 415 kilometres south of the state capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>. Hindi and Mewari are its major languages. The 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> gave the city 451,100 inhabitants. Its economy rests on tourism, agriculture and mineral industries, and it is known for marble, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, paintings and terracotta. Visitors come for the lakes, palaces, forts and temples, and for festivals such as Gangaur, which women celebrate in the month of Chaitra and whose idols are immersed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-pichola">Lake Pichola</a> from the Gangaur Ghat.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Udaipur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mewar, known in its last centuries as <b>Udaipur</b> State, was the kingdom of the Guhila dynasty in the hills and valleys of southern Rajasthan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When British rule ended in August 1947, Rajputana consisted of some two dozen princely states and chiefships, among them ancient Rajput kingdoms such as Mewar (<b>Udaipur</b>), Marwar (Jodhpur), Jaipur and Bikaner, together with the small British province of Ajmer-Merwara.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-state-of-rajasthan">United State of Rajasthan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Chhota <b>Udaipur</b> emerged as a separate state around 1743.</blockquote><a href="/state/chhota-udaipur-state">Chhota Udaipur State</a></li><li><blockquote>The Paramaras also built the Udayeshvara temple at <b>Udaipur</b> in Malwa.</blockquote><a href="/state/paramara-dynasty">Paramara dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Udaipur</b> was a dependency of Sarguja in the Chota Nagpur hills and came under British supervision around 1818 with the other states taken from the Nagpur Kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/udaipur-state-chhattisgarh">Udaipur State (Chhattisgarh)</a></li></ul></section>
